<% content_for :title, "Getting Started with Rails on Build" %>

<main class="container">
  <header>
    <div class="badge">⬢ Running on Build</div>
    <h1><%= @greeting %></h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      This is a sample Ruby on Rails app deployed on
      <strong>Build</strong>. Clone it, change it, and redeploy —
      it's your playground.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Runtime info</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Ruby version</th><td><%= @env[:ruby_version] %></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Rails version</th><td><%= @env[:rails_version] %></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Listening on port</th><td><%= @env[:port] %></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Deployed at</th><td><%= @env[:deployed_at] %></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Uptime</th><td><%= @env[:uptime] %>s</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="hint">
      The port comes from the <code>PORT</code> environment variable that
      Build injects at runtime.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Live config vars</h2>
    <p>
      The greeting above is read from the <code>GREETING</code> config var.
      Change it without redeploying:
    </p>
    <pre><code>bld config:set GREETING="こんにちは, Build!" -a &lt;your-app&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>Then refresh this page, or hit the API:</p>
    <pre><code>curl https://&lt;your-app&gt;.build.io/api/greeting</code></pre>
    <p class="hint" id="api-result">Fetching <code>/api/status</code>…</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Make it yours</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add a new route in <code>config/routes.rb</code> and a matching controller action.</li>
      <li>Or edit this page's content in <code>app/views/pages/index.html.erb</code>.</li>
      <li>Commit, push to GitHub, and watch Build redeploy automatically.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <a href="https://build.io">build.io</a> ·
    <a href="/api/status">/api/status</a> ·
    <a href="/api/greeting">/api/greeting</a>
  </footer>
</main>

<script>
  fetch('/api/status')
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(d => {
      document.getElementById('api-result').textContent =
        `Live from /api/status — status: ${d.status}, uptime: ${d.uptimeSeconds}s, ruby: ${d.ruby}`;
    })
    .catch(() => {
      document.getElementById('api-result').textContent =
        'Could not reach /api/status';
    });
</script>
